{"actor":"director","data":{"action":"start","to":"experiment"},"hash":"bd3b2c9d295c6b0e020f1eb21e5dde9fc1d926fa5b2b370e48cb6db4bd4ef128","kind":"stage","prev":"2bde0f5e87919f6fa3c6c00ebb4fc726d753ee90cc1d7eddf928a94cab3df001","seq":23,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T03:22:53.642+00:00"}
{"actor":"experiment","data":{"control":"knn","datasets":["breast_cancer","breast_cancer_imb10","digits_binary","digits_binary_imb10","wine_binary"],"folds":5,"hypothesis_id":"H1","id":"D1","metrics":["accuracy"],"model":"knn","rationale":"The hypothesis focuses on comparing Z-score and Min-Max scaling for improving KNN classification accuracy on tabular data with skewed feature distributions. To test this, we apply KNN with different scaling methods across datasets that vary in class distribution (original vs. imbalanced) and feature dimensionality. The control condition uses KNN without scaling to establish baseline performance. The treatment conditions test the effect of Z-score and Min-Max scaling on accuracy. We use paired Wilcoxon test to compare the accuracy between Z-score and Min-Max scaling, as it is appropriate for non-parametric comparisons of paired observations. Cross-validation (5 folds) ensures robustness, and multiple seeds ensure reproducibility and variance estimation. The inclusion of imbalanced datasets (e.g., breast_cancer_imb10, digits_binary_imb10) ensures the test reflects real-world skewed feature distributions. The metrics focus on accuracy as the dependent variable specified in the hypothesis. The design controls for model type (KNN), dataset size, and structure, isolating the effect of scaling method.","seeds":[123,456,789],"split":"stratified_kfold","test":"paired_wilcoxon","treatments":["knn+standardize","knn+z-score","knn+min-max"]},"hash":"a3fa0c590d536371b5f9e04f9368440b0ec004e4b20ce8f65510f1e54e20682a","kind":"artefact","prev":"bd3b2c9d295c6b0e020f1eb21e5dde9fc1d926fa5b2b370e48cb6db4bd4ef128","seq":24,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T03:22:53.642+00:00"}
{"actor":"experiment","data":{"control":"knn","datasets":["breast_cancer_imb10","digits_binary_imb10"],"folds":5,"hypothesis_id":"H2","id":"D2","metrics":["precision","recall"],"model":"knn","rationale":"The hypothesis focuses on whether feature scaling has a significant effect on precision in KNN classification when outliers are present. To test this, we select datasets with high outlier presence and class imbalance—specifically, the 'imb10' variants of 'breast_cancer' and 'digits_binary'—as these are known to exhibit sensitivity to outliers and imbalanced distributions. The control condition (knn) evaluates performance without scaling, while the treatment (knn+standardize) applies standardization to assess its impact. Precision is the primary dependent variable as specified in the hypothesis. A paired Wilcoxon test is used because it is appropriate for non-normal distributions and compares related samples (same model on same data with and without scaling). Five-fold cross-validation ensures robustness, and seeds 123 and 456 ensure reproducibility. This design directly addresses the gap G2 by evaluating precision under outlier-prone, imbalanced conditions, which are not captured in existing evidence limited to accuracy and ROC AUC.","seeds":[123,456],"split":"stratified_kfold","test":"paired_wilcoxon","treatments":["knn+standardize"]},"hash":"c9718ef177505c1715817a4d0c07881e435e916c0540a7c41dfc564287435bbf","kind":"artefact","prev":"a3fa0c590d536371b5f9e04f9368440b0ec004e4b20ce8f65510f1e54e20682a","seq":25,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T03:22:53.642+00:00"}
